    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Jehovah's Witnesses are a nontrinitarian, millenarian and restorationist Christian denomination that grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> Student movement of the nineteenth century. In 1870 Charles Taze Russell and others formed a group in Pittsburgh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, to study the Bible, and Russell came to dispute several tenets of mainstream <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, among them the immortality of the soul, hellfire, predestination and the Trinity. In 1876 he met Nelson H. Barbour, and that year they produced the book Three Worlds, which taught that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> had begun the "harvest of the Gospel age" by an invisible return in 1874 and that 1914 would end a 2,520-year period called "the Gentile Times". The two split over doctrine in June 1879, and in July Russell began publishing Zion's Watch Tower and Herald of Christ's Presence.</p><p>From 1879 Watch Tower supporters gathered as autonomous congregations, and thirty were founded. Zion's Watch Tower Tract Society was co-founded in 1881 and incorporated in 1884 as a nonprofit business to distribute tracts and Bibles. Russell moved its headquarters to Brooklyn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City, in 1909 and identified the movement as the Bible Students. By 1910 about 50,000 people worldwide were associated with it. He died on 31 October 1916, aged 64.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Jehovah's Witnesses believe that Jehovah, the form of God's name they prefer, is the only true god and the creator of all things, and that he is not part of a Trinity. They hold that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> is God's only direct creation and his "only-begotten Son", the first angel, who died on a single upright post rather than a cross, and that the Holy Spirit is God's applied power or "active force". They believe death is a state of nonexistence, reject a hell of fiery torment, and hold that a "little flock" of 144,000 will go to heaven to rule with Jesus while the majority are resurrected to a cleansed earth after Armageddon.</p><p>They believe that Jesus began to rule invisibly in heaven as king in October 1914, that the present world system will soon be destroyed at Armageddon, and that the earth will then become a paradise. They regard all other religions as false, identifying them with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> the Great, and consider human governments to be controlled by Satan. They do not observe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a>, birthdays or other holidays and customs they consider to have pagan origins. They are told to reject the transfusion of whole blood, a prohibition that has existed since 1945, based on their reading of Acts 15:28, 29.</p><p>Worship takes place at Kingdom Halls, which contain no religious symbols, and since 2009 congregations meet twice a week. The Memorial of Christ's death, on the fourteenth day of the Jewish month Nisan, is the most important annual event, and only those who consider themselves anointed partake of the bread and wine.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Jehovah's Witnesses consider the entire Protestant canon the inspired, inerrant word of God, interpret much of it literally while accepting parts as symbolic, and are old Earth creationists. They prefer their own translation, the New World Translation of the Holy Scriptures, which Nathan Knorr commissioned and which was released in full in 1961. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> scholars including Bruce M. Metzger and MacLean Gilmour have said that scholarship is evident in it but that its rendering of certain texts is inaccurate and biased in favour of Witness practices and doctrines; other critics call it scholastically dishonest. Most of the criticism concerns the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>, especially the introduction of the name Jehovah and passages related to the Trinity.</p><p>The movement's teaching has come largely through periodicals and books. Russell published a six-book series, Studies in the Scriptures, and by 1912 was the most distributed Christian author in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. In 1917 Rutherford released The Finished Mystery as a seventh volume, which he claimed to be Russell's posthumous work but which Clayton Woodworth, George Fisher and Gertrude Seibert had written. In 2010 The Watchtower and Awake! were the world's most widely distributed magazines, and the group's website jw.org replaced watchtower.org in 2008.</p><p>The Governing Body issues no single statement of faith and states its positions through Watch Tower Society publications, which teach that doctrinal changes come through "progressive revelation" and "new light". Members are discouraged from reaching "private ideas" through Bible research independent of these publications.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under Russell the movement grew from the thirty congregations of 1879 and 1880. By about 1900 he had organised thousands of part- and full-time colporteurs, was appointing foreign missionaries and establishing branch offices, and by the 1910s his organisation kept nearly a hundred "pilgrims", or travelling preachers. About 50,000 people worldwide were associated with the movement by 1910.</p><p>Under Rutherford tens of thousands of defections occurred in the first half of his tenure, leading to several independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> Student organisations, the largest of which was the Dawn Bible Students Association. By mid-1919 an estimated one in seven of Russell-era Bible Students had ceased their association with the Society, and between 1921 and 1931 three-quarters were estimated to have left, although Alan Rogerson believes the total number to be unclear. Under Knorr, who became president in 1942, international assemblies, training programmes, missionary activity and branch offices expanded worldwide.</p><p>From 1966 publications and convention talks built anticipation that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>' thousand-year reign might begin in 1975 or shortly thereafter. Baptisms rose from about 59,000 in 1966 to more than 297,000 in 1974, and by 1975 the number of active members exceeded two million. Membership declined significantly for a few years after the prediction failed. In 2025 Jehovah's Witnesses reported about 9 million publishers in about 119,500 congregations and a worldwide annual increase of 2.5%.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Jehovah's Witnesses are organised hierarchically in what the leadership calls a theocratic organisation, directed by the Governing Body, an all-male group that establishes all doctrines. Branch offices, each also called Bethel, manage activities in countries or regions, and volunteers called Bethelites live and work there. Each congregation has unpaid male elders and ministerial servants appointed under a traveling overseer; the denomination does not use elder as a title for a formal clergy-laity division. Only men may hold positions of authority. Funding is largely donated by members, with no tithing or collection.</p><p>Members are expected to preach, often in pairs, and to submit a monthly report; those who do not report for six consecutive months are termed "inactive". Baptism, which they consider required for salvation, is not practised on infants. Members who commit serious offences may be expelled and shunned, and so are those who formally leave, who are termed "disassociated". Most limit their social interaction with non-Witnesses, whom they regard as living in a world influenced by Satan.</p><p>The denomination has been variously described as a church, sect, new religious movement or cult, and academics generally stopped using the term cult in the 1980s because of its pejorative associations. Alan Rogerson describes the leadership as totalitarian, which James Irvin Lichti rejects. A 2008 US Pew Forum survey found that about 37% of people raised in the group continued to identify as Jehovah's Witnesses, and that 65% of adult American Witnesses are converts.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The refusal of military service and of saluting flags and anthems has brought the group into conflict with several governments. In 1918 Watch Tower Society directors were jailed for sedition under the Espionage Act after The Finished Mystery criticised clergy and Christian involvement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">Great War</a>, and members were subjected to mob violence; the directors were released in March 1919 and the charges dropped in 1920. In 1933 there were approximately 20,000 Witnesses in Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, of whom about 10,000 were imprisoned. About 2,000 were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>, where they were identified by purple triangles, and as many as 1,200 died, including 250 who were executed. Unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a>, who were persecuted for their ethnicity, Witnesses could escape persecution by signing a document renouncing their faith.</p><p>Their activities are banned or restricted in some countries, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and many Muslim-majority countries, and members have been imprisoned for refusing conscription. Their legal challenges have influenced civil rights legislation in several countries.</p><p>The group has also drawn criticism for unfulfilled predictions about 1914, 1925 and 1975. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> ended its state funding of the community because of its shunning policy, concluding that shunning, particularly of children, constitutes psychological violence. Its policies on child sexual abuse have been examined in inquiries, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>'s Royal Commission in 2015 and an inquiry by the Charity Commission for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> begun in 2017; the organisation maintains that the best way to protect children is to educate parents.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Jehovah's Witnesses are a nontrinitarian Christian denomination that grew from Charles Taze Russell's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> Students and took its name in 1931 under Joseph Rutherford. Known for evangelism, refusal of military service and of whole-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blood-transfusion">blood transfusions</a>, they reported about 9.2 million members in 2025 and were persecuted in Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</em></p>

    <section class="ty-sec" id="test-yourself" aria-labelledby="ty-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="ty-title">Test yourself</h2>
